  Because the player in case 3 was also explicitly informed that
a cancellation attack was taking place.  (As you pointed out
yourself.)

  Is that sufficient to justify it?  Maybe yes, maybe no.  


  Stunning, confusing, and scaring effects are also reported
by Magicbane, as well as monsters resisting those effects.
(At least by my current reading of artifact.c.)

  So if Magicbane didn't report when Magicbane's cancellation
effect was resisted, the lack of that report would be 
inconsistent with Magicbane's other reporting behavior.

  The other difference is physical contact (indirectly through
Magicbane) as opposed to action at a distance.



